            <ArticleTitle>PERK leads a hub dictating pancreatic β cell homoeostasis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/boc.201700059</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In humans, the pathogenesis of diabetes is characterised by two major pancreatic β cell defects: a reduction in β cell mass and the failure of β cells to produce enough insulin. Over the past two decades, multiple studies involving cell cultures, animal models and human subjects have established the importance of the protein kinase RNA-like endoplasmic reticulum kinase (PERK) in the adaptive functional capacity of pancreatic β cells during embryonic development and into adulthood. In this review, we will highlight major findings identifying PERK as a crucial player in β cell physiology and in diabetes.</AbstractText>
